assert — Stop if a condition is false                                        core · tier 0 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · exact

USAGE
    assert(condition, message)

DESCRIPTION
    Checks a result and stops the script with `message` when the check fails: the error is Z0001, the code
    of `error`, and the exit code is 1 (section 3.8 of the Language Reference). When the check holds,
    nothing is printed and the script carries on, so an `assert` costs nothing to read and guards a re-run:
    a row count after a filter, a share between 0 and 1, a coefficient with the expected sign.
    `assert(equal(f(x), [1, 2]), "...")` is how a project tests its own functions.

    The condition is one Bool. `NA` is neither true nor false and is an error (Z0203), as it is for `if`, so
    a check on a value that may be missing tests `is_missing` first; a column is not one Bool either
    (Z0203): reduce it with `all` or `any`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)`. Like R's `stopifnot`, with
    a message of your own; like Stata's `assert`, which stops with "assertion is false".

ARGUMENTS
    condition   Bool. The check, a Bool.
    message     Str. What to say when it fails.

VALUE
    Nothing.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Z0001   the script stopped itself with the message given

ACCURACY
    Exact.

SEE ALSO
    error, warn, equal, all

EXAMPLES
    > assert(1 + 1 == 2, "arithmetic still holds")
    > let d = frame(x = [1, 2, 3])
    > assert(d.n_rows() == 3, "three rows expected")
